    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The compatibility promise is real and Ryobi has kept it longer than any competitor. What the promise does not cover is performance, and a twenty-year-old pack in a brushless circular saw will teach you that distinction quickly.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every battery carrying the ONE+ badge fits every tool carrying the ONE+ badge. The physical interface and the voltage have not changed since the platform launched in 1996, which means a battery bought this year drops into a drill bought nearly thirty years ago, and a blue nickel-cadmium pack from that era physically fits a brand new brushless impact driver.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is not an accident or a happy side effect; it is a deliberate and heavily marketed commitment, and Ryobi has maintained it across three battery chemistries and several generations of tool electronics. No other major cordless platform has kept a single battery interface going that long, and it is the main reason people stay on ONE+ once they own a few packs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The badge is what matters. Look for ONE+ on both the tool and the battery. Ryobi sells other platforms that are not compatible with this one, and confusing them is the single most common purchasing error, which the section on incompatible products covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the compatibility answer is genuinely simple. The complications are all about performance, charging and whether an old pack can usefully do what a new tool asks of it, and those are worth understanding before you buy a mower on the strength of the batteries in your drawer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE PROMISE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Any ONE+ battery in any ONE+ tool. Physically, always.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">18 volts, one interface, since 1996, across nickel-cadmium, standard lithium and HP lithium. What changes across generations is how much current a pack can deliver, which decides whether a high-draw tool performs well or shuts down under load.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="history" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Blue, Green and HP: Three Generations of the Same Platform
          </h2>

          <p>
            The colour of the pack tells you roughly when it was made and what chemistry is inside, which in turn predicts how it behaves in a modern tool.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blue: Nickel-Cadmium, Roughly 1996 to 2007</h3>

          <p>
            The original ONE+ packs. Heavy, low capacity by modern standards, prone to the self-discharge and memory behaviour that nickel-cadmium is known for, and containing cadmium, which is why the chemistry has largely disappeared. They still fit every ONE+ tool. Whether they are worth using is a different question, since most surviving blue packs are long past their useful life.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Green: Lithium-Ion, Roughly 2007 Onward</h3>

          <p>
            The transition to lithium brought much higher energy density, far less self-discharge, no memory effect, and a large jump in usable capacity. These are the standard ONE+ packs most people own, in capacities from 1.5 to 6.0 amp-hours, and they run the whole range of ONE+ tools competently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">HP: High Performance Lithium, Roughly 2020 Onward</h3>

          <p>
            Still 18 volts, still ONE+, still the same physical interface. What changed is the cells and the internal electronics, which allow substantially higher continuous current delivery. These are the packs designed to feed the brushless HP tools, and they are the reason a modern ONE+ chainsaw or circular saw is a genuinely usable tool rather than a novelty.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Generation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chemistry</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Capacity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fits All ONE+ Tools</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Blue</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nickel-cadmium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.2-2.4 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Green standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lithium-ion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5-6.0 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Green HP</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High-output lithium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.0-12.0 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lithium+ / HP High Capacity</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High-output lithium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6.0-12.0 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Note that voltage never changed. Every one of these is an 18-volt pack, and anyone telling you an HP battery is higher voltage has misunderstood the naming. HP describes current delivery and cell quality, not volts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fits-vs-performs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Where Fitting and Performing Diverge
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the part the compatibility promise does not cover, and it is what people actually run into.
          </p>

          <p>
            A tool's performance depends on how much current the battery can deliver, not just on voltage. A brushless circular saw cutting through a joist draws a great deal of current, and a small or old pack simply cannot supply it. The tool responds by running slowly, bogging down in the cut, getting hot, or triggering its own protection circuit and shutting off. None of that is a fault; it is the pack reaching its limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the practical hierarchy is this. Low-draw tools — drills, drivers, lights, small vacuums, glue guns, inflators — run acceptably on almost any ONE+ pack including old ones. Medium-draw tools such as circular saws, reciprocating saws and impact wrenches want at least a 4.0 amp-hour standard pack. High-draw tools like chainsaws, lawnmowers, blowers and larger saws genuinely want HP packs, and ideally high-capacity ones.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tool Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Minimum Sensible Pack</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ideal</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drill, driver, light</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5 Ah standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.0-4.0 Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Impact driver</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.0 Ah standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.0 Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Circular saw, recip saw</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.0 Ah standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP 4.0+ Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Impact wrench</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4.0 Ah standard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP 4.0+ Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chainsaw, pole saw</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP 4.0 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP 6.0-12.0 Ah</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lawn mower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP 6.0 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP 12.0 Ah or two packs</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The HP tools deserve a specific note. A brushless ONE+ HP tool will run on a standard green pack and will work, but it cannot reach its rated performance, because the motor can accept more current than the pack can supply. Pairing an HP tool with an HP battery is where the marketing claims about performance actually come from, and the difference in a demanding cut is obvious rather than marginal.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reverse is entirely fine and is worth knowing: an HP battery in an old non-brushless drill works perfectly, and often runs cooler and longer than the pack that came with it. There is no downside to using a better battery in an older tool beyond the weight and the cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="chargers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Chargers: The Real Compatibility Trap
          </h2>

          <p>
            If something in a ONE+ setup is going to be incompatible, it is almost always the charger rather than the battery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nickel-cadmium and lithium-ion require completely different charging behaviour. A charger designed only for the old blue nickel-cadmium packs does not have the circuitry to charge lithium correctly, and should not be used to try. Lithium cells need controlled constant-current then constant-voltage charging with cell monitoring, which an old dumb charger simply does not do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi addressed this with dual chemistry chargers, which detect what has been inserted and charge it appropriately. Most chargers sold in the lithium era handle both, and they are usually marked as dual chemistry or list both chemistries on the label. If you have inherited an old charger with a set of tools, check the label before putting a lithium pack on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charge time varies enormously and is worth understanding before buying. A standard charger might take several hours for a large pack, while a rapid charger can cut that dramatically. For anyone with more than two or three batteries, a rapid charger changes how the whole system feels to use far more than another battery does.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical point about older packs on modern chargers: a deeply discharged lithium pack that has sat for years may not accept a charge at all, and the charger may indicate a fault. That usually means the cells have dropped below the voltage at which the protection circuit will allow charging, and it is generally the end of that pack rather than a charger problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store packs at a partial charge rather than empty or full if they will sit for months, keep them out of freezing sheds and hot cars, and they will last considerably longer. Our guide on how long Ryobi batteries last covers storage and lifespan in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="amp-hours" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Amp-Hours, and Why Bigger Is Not Always Better
          </h2>

          <p>
            Amp-hours measure capacity — how long a pack runs — and not power. A 6.0 amp-hour pack does not make a drill more powerful than a 2.0; it makes it run roughly three times as long.
          </p>

          <p>
            That said, larger packs generally do deliver more current as well, because they contain more cells in parallel and each cell is therefore working less hard. So a bigger battery often does improve performance in a demanding tool, but the mechanism is current capability rather than capacity, and that is why HP packs matter more than raw amp-hours for the hardest jobs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is weight and balance. A 12.0 amp-hour pack on a compact drill turns a pleasant one-handed tool into something awkward and nose-heavy, and for overhead work it is genuinely tiring. Most people are better served by a 2.0 on the drill and a 4.0 or 6.0 on the saws than by one enormous pack they move between everything.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Capacity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx Weight</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1.5 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~0.8 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lights, inflators, occasional drilling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2.0 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.0 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drills and drivers, everyday use</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4.0 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1.8 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Saws, sanders, general workshop</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">6.0 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2.4 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High-draw tools, longer sessions</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">9.0-12.0 Ah</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~3.5-4.5 lb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mowers, chainsaws, outdoor equipment</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A sensible household set is two small packs for the drill and driver, and two larger HP packs for anything that cuts or mows, with a rapid charger. That covers nearly every domestic job without ever carrying more weight than the task needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our guide on cordless tool batteries explains the volts, amp-hours and platform relationship in general terms if you want the underlying concepts rather than the Ryobi specifics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="not-compatible" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Is Not Compatible
          </h2>

          <p>
            The compatibility promise applies to ONE+ and only to ONE+, and Ryobi sells several things that are not it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 40V platform is entirely separate. It is Ryobi's outdoor power equipment line — larger mowers, snow blowers, heavier chainsaws — and its batteries are physically and electrically incompatible with ONE+. They do not fit, and no adapter should be used to try. This is the single most common mix-up when buying, and our comparison of the 18V and 40V platforms covers which to choose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ryobi's older 12V and 14.4V tools, and the various specialist lines that have come and gone, are likewise separate. If the tool or battery does not say ONE+, assume it is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other brands are not compatible, obviously, but it is worth stating because adapters exist. Third-party adapters that let a DeWalt or Milwaukee battery run a Ryobi tool are sold widely, and using them voids warranties, bypasses the communication between pack and tool that manages current limits and thermal protection, and is a genuine fire risk with lithium cells. The saving is not worth it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third-party replacement ONE+ batteries are a more nuanced question. Some are decent and many are not, and the failure modes with cheap lithium packs are not trivial ones. Cells of unknown provenance, protection circuits of unknown quality, and no recourse if something goes wrong. Given how often genuine packs go on offer, the margin is smaller than it looks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be careful with listings that say compatible with Ryobi rather than stating they are Ryobi. That phrasing almost always indicates a third-party pack, and the price usually confirms it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strategy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. A Buying Strategy That Uses the Compatibility Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            The whole point of a platform this backward-compatible is that you buy batteries once and tools many times, so it is worth being deliberate about it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy bare tools wherever possible. Once you have three or four working packs, every kit you buy with batteries included is money spent on batteries you do not need. Ryobi sells a very wide range of bare tools precisely because their customers accumulate packs, and the price difference is substantial.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy HP packs from here on even if your current tools are standard. They work in everything, they run cooler, and they mean that when you eventually buy a brushless saw or a chainsaw you are not buying batteries again alongside it. The premium over a standard pack is smaller than the cost of discovering later that your packs cannot feed the new tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for holiday bundle deals, which in this category are genuinely good. Ryobi routinely offers a free tool with a battery kit, and the effective price of a bare tool through that route is often lower than buying it outright.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not chase the biggest pack. Two 4.0 amp-hour packs are more useful than one 8.0, because you can charge one while using the other, and because a lighter pack on a drill is a better tool. Redundancy beats capacity in a domestic setting.
          </p>

          <p>
            And keep the old packs working rather than replacing them. A tired 1.5 amp-hour battery is still perfectly good for the work light, the inflator and the glue gun, and relegating it to those jobs frees your good packs for the tools that need them.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
