        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing ONE+ With the 40V Platform</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ryobi's 40V outdoor line is entirely separate and its batteries are physically and electrically incompatible with 18V ONE+. This is the most common purchasing error in the range. Check that both the tool and the battery carry the ONE+ badge, and never attempt to adapt between the two.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting an Old Pack to Run a Brushless HP Tool Properly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Compatibility is about fit, not performance. A high-draw tool such as a chainsaw or circular saw needs a pack that can deliver high continuous current, and an old or small battery will bog down, run hot or trigger its own protection and shut off. That is the pack reaching its limit, not a faulty tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Lithium Pack on an Old Nickel-Cadmium Charger</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The two chemistries need completely different charging behaviour, and an old blue-era charger lacks the circuitry to charge lithium correctly. Check the label for dual chemistry marking before using an inherited charger. Most chargers sold in the lithium era handle both, but not all of them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Kits With Batteries Once You Already Have Several</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The platform's whole value is that you buy batteries once and tools repeatedly. Once you own three or four working packs, every included battery is money wasted. Ryobi sells an unusually wide range of bare tools for exactly this reason, and the price gap is substantial.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Third-Party Cross-Brand Battery Adapters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Adapters letting a DeWalt or Milwaukee pack run a Ryobi tool bypass the communication between battery and tool that manages current limits and thermal protection. With lithium cells that is a genuine fire risk as well as a warranty void, and the saving does not justify it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are all Ryobi ONE+ batteries interchangeable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every battery carrying the ONE+ badge fits every tool carrying it, going back to the original blue nickel-cadmium packs from 1996. The voltage and physical interface have never changed across three chemistries. What varies is how much current a pack can deliver, which decides how well a demanding tool performs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do old blue Ryobi batteries work in new tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Physically yes, they fit every ONE+ tool. Practically they are poor in anything demanding, because nickel-cadmium delivers less current and most surviving blue packs are long past their useful life. They are fine for a work light or an inflator and unsuitable for a brushless saw.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Ryobi ONE+ and ONE+ HP batteries interchangeable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in both directions. HP packs are still 18-volt ONE+ batteries with the same interface; what differs is higher-output cells and electronics. An HP pack works perfectly in an older tool and often runs cooler. A standard pack works in an HP tool but cannot let it reach its rated performance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a Ryobi 40V battery fit a ONE+ tool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The 40V platform is Ryobi's separate outdoor power equipment line, and its batteries are physically and electrically incompatible with 18V ONE+. There is no adapter that should be used between them. Check both tool and battery for the ONE+ badge before buying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need HP batteries for HP tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                To get the performance you paid for, yes. A brushless HP tool runs on a standard green pack but cannot draw the current its motor can accept, so it underperforms in demanding work. In a hard cut the difference is obvious rather than marginal. For drills and drivers, a standard pack is perfectly adequate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I charge a lithium Ryobi battery on an old charger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the charger is marked as dual chemistry. Chargers made for the original nickel-cadmium packs lack the circuitry lithium requires and should not be used for it. Most chargers from the lithium era handle both chemistries, but check the label on any charger inherited with old tools.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a bigger amp-hour battery more powerful?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not directly — amp-hours measure how long a pack runs, not how hard it can push. Larger packs do often deliver more current because they contain more cells sharing the load, which helps in demanding tools, but for the hardest jobs the HP designation matters more than raw capacity. Bigger packs are also heavier and unbalance a compact drill.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

